<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-202. Deleting a MIDI clip WORKS, and undo brings back that very clip.

  THE DEFECT WAS A REFUSAL AIMED AT SOMETHING ELSE. QBX-146 stopped
  SRemoveSampleAction from deleting a clip it could build no inverse for -
  the conservative half of that fix, and the right call: before it, the delete
  succeeded, pushed nothing on the undo stack, and the next Ctrl+Z popped an
  unrelated command, so "undo appears to work while the content stays gone".
  Only three shapes had an inverse: file-backed (re-add the sample),
  container-backed (rebuild the cut over the same track) and a take column
  (re-link the pinned object). Everything else was refused.

  A MIDI clip is none of the three. Its content is an SMidiSequence, which is
  not an SExternFile and not a path container, and a single clip is not a take
  column - so `remove-sample` refused it, and the Delete key on a recorded
  MIDI clip did nothing but write a warning. The refusal's own message named a
  live SRecordingContent as what reaches it, which by then was no longer true.

  WHAT MAKES THE FIX SAFE RATHER THAN A RELAXED GUARD. The pin path does not
  rebuild anything: it addRef()s the object, deletes the link, and the inverse
  re-links THE SAME OBJECT, so every field survives by identity - the notes,
  their lengths, the per-clip transpose and velocity scale, the channel
  override, the link's timebase. Nothing can be forgotten by a parameter list
  because there is no parameter list. That is why the pin is tried before the
  two rebuild branches, and why widening it to every content EXCEPT a live
  recording is the fix rather than a workaround.

  A LIVE RECORDING STAYS REFUSED, for ownership and not for fidelity:
  SAudioRecorder::Armed holds the growing SCut and its SLink as borrowed raw
  pointers and deletes them at stop, so removing that clip is a use-after-free
  in the recorder. live_recording_delete_refused.qxa is that half, and it is
  what stops this widening from reaching the one shape that must not move.
  The MIDI recorder has no such exposure by construction - it buffers messages
  and submits `place-midi-recording` once, at the stop, so no growing MIDI
  clip is ever in the model to delete.

  NOT GATED HERE: the third link field, eventChannelOverride_. It is carried
  with the other two and for the same reason, but nothing can observe it on a
  shape that reaches the pin. `set-clip-event-channel` refuses a placement that
  is not an SCut, and a MIDI clip's placement object is an SMidiCut; the only
  shape that can hold an override AND take the pin path is a WRAPPED take
  column, and no verb reports the value back - it is observable only through
  the lane's exported event feed, which an audio column has none of. The carry
  is one line mirroring merge-columns' carryLaneLinkAttrs(); a gate for it
  wants a verb that reports a placement's override.

  Frames per tick: 120 BPM, PPQ 960, 48 kHz -> 25 frames per tick, so
  3840 ticks = 96000 frames and tick 960 = frame 24000.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="midi_clip_delete_undo_restores">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="0" timePos="0" duration="3840" name="Notes"/>

    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="0" dur="1920" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="960" dur="480" key="64" velocity="80" channel="0"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="2"/>

    <!-- PER-CLIP MODIFIERS, so that a restore which rebuilt a lookalike from
         captured parameters would be visibly wrong rather than plausibly
         right. These live on the SMidiCut window, not in the sequence, and
         they are exactly the kind of field a parameter list forgets. -->
    <set-midi-cut clip="0,0" transpose="12" velocityScale="0.5" channel="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="1" key="72" at="0"
                        velocity="50" channel="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="1" key="76" at="24000"
                        velocity="40" channel="1"/>
    <!-- Notes are stored WITH their length: 1920 ticks = 48000 frames. -->
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" contains="dur=48000"/>

    <!-- AND ONE FIELD THAT LIVES ON THE LINK RATHER THAN ON THE OBJECT, which
         is the half the pin's identity does NOT cover: the inverse builds a
         new SLink, and a new link over event content is born on BEATS
         (SLink::defaultTimebaseFor). So a clip deliberately switched to TIME
         is the discriminator - it comes back on beats unless the forward
         captured the link's own fields and the inverse applied them. Beats
         would have been restored by accident. -->
    <set-link-timebase clip="0,0" timebase="time"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000"
                        timebase="time"/>

    <!-- THE DELETE. This is what the ticket reported as impossible: before
         the fix it is refused, the clip is still there, and the assertion
         below fails. -->
    <remove-sample trackPath="0" clipIndex="0" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- ...and the refusal is not merely absent: the clip is GONE. An
         unresolvable path is what assert-clip-window rejects on, which is how
         a case asserts an absence with it. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" expectReject="true"/>

    <undo count="1"/>

    <!-- RESTORED BY IDENTITY. Both notes, both lengths, and both per-clip
         modifiers - a rebuilt clip would come back at transpose 0 and
         velocity scale 1, i.e. key 60 at velocity 100 on channel 0. -->
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="2"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="1" key="72" at="0"
                        velocity="50" channel="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="1" key="76" at="24000"
                        velocity="40" channel="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" contains="dur=48000"/>
    <!-- The window came back too, at its own position and length - and on
         the TIMEBASE IT WAS GIVEN, not the one a fresh link would default to.
         -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000"
                        timebase="time"/>

    <!-- And the inverse of the inverse holds. -->
    <redo count="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- One more undo, so the case does not end on a destroyed clip: a pin
         released by the undo stack's teardown is the path that collects the
         content, and it is worth exercising with the clip restored. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events clip="0,0" kind="note" count="2"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
